@PascalPrecht thanks for confirming. After I found out that it works with `null` it got that suspicion, but I don't find this pretty. 
In Polymer when the property of type boolean is bound to an attribute, it treats it as boolean attribute. I find this a better approach. 
If I want the binding result in `<div hidden="true">` I can use a string property with value `"true"` or `"false"`.

issue.body:
It appears that in browsers setting the href property to any value (even null) on an anchor element will stringify it (and sync with the attribute). Ex `a = document.createElement('a'); a.href = null; -> <a href="null">`

This means that `<a [href]="null">` will be decorated as a link for 'null'. For users that want to optionally decorate their links depending on presence of some data, the only option left is using `ng-if`.

Adding simple sugar to generate the following setter 

```
(el, val) => {
   if (val !== null) el.href = val;
   else el.removeAttribute('href');
}
```

will make href binding a lot more useful. The downside is having custom logic in the compiler. Thoughts?
